why 1 1/2 PROGRESSION The bass walks down C–B♭–A–G–F–E♭ by step; the chords over it are passing ones, not a step backward.
why 3 3/4 SIMILAR_MOTION All voices rise into ii°⁶ before the half cadence, the bass by a fourth. Each line moves by its own interval.
why 7 1/4 CROSSING The alto falls D–C while the tenor leaps up to F, so they trade places for a beat. The tenor then steps down E♭–D.
why 7 1/4 LEAPS The alto falls F♯–D–C toward the F chord. The tritone F♯–C is spread over three notes and two chords.
why 7 3/8 CROSSING The tenor steps down to E♭, still above the alto's C; on the next beat the alto leaps up to F and is on top again.
why 7 1/2 DOUBLING Soprano (C–D) and tenor (E♭–D) meet on D from opposite sides by step.
why 9 1/4 DOUBLING The tune falls F–D while the tenor steps C–D; both land on D, the third of the B♭ chord.
why 11 0 DOUBLING Soprano (C–D) and alto (E♭–D) meet on D by step from opposite sides.
why 11 1/2 DOUBLING The alto's F♯ rises to G as a leading tone should, and the tenor steps A–G; both reach G. A correct resolution beats the ideal doubling.
